# TODO - Authenticated Blooket reads

## Objective

Finish verified questions, capabilities, pagination, and browser-session stops
without presenting incomplete reads as publication-ready state.

## Acceptance

Complete when fresh validated remote state can be read reliably, capability
changes are detected, and a missing/challenged session returns the appropriate
stop instead of fabricated data or a mutation. These reads establish the
baselines needed for publication and conflict detection.

## Implementation notes

Implement concrete macOS session, capability, My Sets, and set-detail surfaces
behind their existing ports. Reuse the teacher's confirmed browser session and
validate account capabilities, question/media reads, and navigation states
through the existing IR decoders. Keep unknown limits unknown.

Connect ordinary saved email and the secret-store credential boundary when login
is needed. Preserve CAPTCHA, organization selection, unfamiliar login, loading,
expired-session, and human-stop states. Session data and cookies must remain
inside the browser/security boundary.

The extension discovers the workspace automatically through its isolated local
page relay. It never asks the teacher for an address, pairing code, Blooket
credentials, or a Connect action. The popup shows state and page shortcuts.

Jig reports only the existing external `JIG-RULE-GAP-001`. Package-release
freshness evidence was re-fetched from its configured registry providers.

Payload decoding rejects unknown fields, credentials, paths, wrong types,
control characters, and oversized IDs before service/browser access.

Published read commands reuse the existing session without retrieving secrets
or submitting a login. Signed-out or expired sessions return
`blooket-authentication-required`; rate limits, organization prompts, and
challenges preserve their wait/human states. Internal session establishment may
submit configured credentials only after an explicit signed-out/expired
observation, and it still requires a fresh post-submit observation before
reporting readiness.

Missing or stale local instances fail closed. Local requests use exact loopback
discovery, bounded responses, correlation checks, CSRF admission, and no
redirects.

This was a local tool projection check, not a new public OAuth
acceptance run.

The development service restarted normally, the workspace reconnected without
configuration, and the configured tunnel returned to connected. Native Safari,
complete question-level media facts, capability/login live browser acceptance,
and non-empty server-side set-list completeness remain pending. Their absence
still
blocks publication; do not expose unsupported read or write tools as if
validated.

## Evidence

Portable question-read foundations were added on 2026-10-06. The original
version-one contract normalizes question number/text, `mc`/`typing`,
randomization, seconds, answers/correct answers, typing match modes, and only
the presence of question image/audio media. It deliberately does not retain
provider media URLs or invent remote question IDs. Authenticated
`listBlooketQuestions` validates the opaque set ID before browser or secret
access and decodes every adapter result before returning it.

The field evidence comes from recovered build
`86784c3d4c38fcd559c92f38fbadd7a160947de2`: edit module 12048
(`page-720bc856ab06b26b.js`, factory
`b0c808b1bcf957f7a974ba0a27269e5c4eca8c3a3be0663f0207fc7ad04f8bb4`) reads
`set.questions` and each question's number, question, image, audio, `qType`,
random flag, and `timeLimit`; dependency module 35211
(`6749-987e303c90e94fbf.js`, factory
`b21ec449a8a5bbc90abd132bb6b08de90881ab45ba3654182a4d90690e98b90d`) also reads
answers, correct answers, and answer types. Neither module has a recorded
factory variant. The previously authorized Chrome observation on the newer build
confirmed that changing a private test question to typing with a 15-second limit
persisted after reload; keep the two builds distinct.

On 2026-10-07, recovered answer rendering established enough additional client
semantics to version this read contract without exposing provider media
identity. Module 46933 exports `` `~` `` as the answer-image marker and
`` `*` `` as the math marker. Module 89770 (factory
`52429dd04e935943f77f422a6e22e7cabafa8ecc24ad8b50fcffc1c24b1e87de`) writes
an inserted answer image as the image marker followed by `e.url`; module 35211
renders an answer as an image when splitting on that marker yields exactly two
parts and uses only the second part as `src`. Neither module has a recorded
factory variant in this build.

Question-read schema version two therefore normalizes each answer to `text`,
`math`, or `image`, carries correctness and typing match mode with that answer,
and represents image content as `null`. The browser adapter validates the exact
observed marker shape, discards the provider image URL before crossing its
boundary, and fails closed on ambiguous marker placement. Math answers retain
only their marker-delimited expression. Version-one text-only observations
remain accepted and migrate to the normalized shape.

Portable regressions prove that image-answer reads cross the application
boundary without provider identity and cannot confirm a text write. Differential
write verification continues to confirm only exact text-only additions with no
question media; image/media writes remain `inconclusive`. This is recovered
client evidence, not a new live Blooket read-back or mutation.

Question write recovery now captures a normalized question-list digest and can
confirm one exact text-only addition only when removing that candidate
reproduces the pre-write collection. Unchanged state is `not-confirmed`; media
writes, concurrent edits, malformed reads, and ambiguous additions remain
`inconclusive`.

The local browser bridge has a versioned request/response contract, bounded
in-memory broker, bearer-authenticated loopback routes, and API port adapters.
The concrete Chrome worker and page extractor now support session observation,
nonempty My Sets summaries, and the observed private set-detail controls.

Authorized Chrome DOM inspection on 2026-10-06 confirmed My Sets article/h3/Edit
link structure and the detail controls: input#title[name="title"],
textarea#desc[name="desc"], and input#private[name="private"] with role=switch.
The observed unchecked switch displays "Private (Only playable by you)".

Recovered edit module 31017 establishes both exact visibility states: private
renders with the inverted switch unchecked and "Private (Only playable by you)",
while public renders with the switch checked and "Public (Playable by
everyone)". The adapter requires the matching visible label and switch state.

Recovered My Sets module 52644 and its captured route HTML establish the true
empty-library state as "You'll need a question set to host!" plus the visible
"Create a Set" action. The adapter returns an empty set list only with both
markers; zero cards alone, folder-empty text, and search-empty text still fail
closed so loading cannot masquerade as an empty account.

The canonical `sets.list` URL strips search/folder query state before
reading.
The set summary and detail readers reject duplicate `id` query parameters,
even if each duplicate has the same value. The canonical set-list decoder
also bounds observed lists to the existing 200-card local browser safety cap
before item traversal; neither check claims a provider collection-size limit.

Because the page boundary can emit an empty array only for that exact recovered
empty-account state, the browser bridge marks only `[]` as `complete`. Non-empty
card lists remain `unknown`: curated module 52644 consumes `sets`, `allSets`,
and
`numSets`, but the server origin of those props is not present in the curated
client chunks, so visible card count is not promoted to full-account truth.

Inspecting the existing synthetic typing question confirmed its visible text,
answer, match mode, and seconds, but did not establish every required question
field. No edits or Save actions were submitted during this inspection.

The unpacked extension compiles with the pinned repository compiler and is
included in native package resources. Synthetic worker tests cover trusted popup
senders, exact loopback configuration, bounded replies, navigation, unsupported
commands, and signed-out page stops. Artifact tests verify emitted JavaScript
imports and the narrow permission set, with no server/secret modules.

Recovered dashboard HTML and module 88289 confirm a visible modal headed "Select
your organization". Session observation now treats that exact visible dialog as
organization-prompt, and set reads stop while it is present. The adapter does
not choose, submit, or infer an organization.

The recovered My Sets client receives one `sets` collection, then filters and
sorts it locally. No cursor, offset, page, limit, load-more control, or
pagination UI appears in the captured route/client modules. The removed server
Flight data means this does not prove the server never truncates, so non-empty
server-side completeness remains an explicit observation gap rather than an
invented pagination contract.

Set-list probes now carry collection completeness explicitly. The concrete
browser boundary marks only the exact recovered empty-account state as
`complete`; non-empty My Sets card lists remain `unknown`, and canonical reads
preserve that fact. Differential Create Set verification refuses to capture or
consume a set-list baseline unless the source declares the collection
`complete`. Synthetic complete-list tests preserve the exact-difference verifier
for a future evidence-backed non-empty source; unknown live lists remain
observational only.

Still pending: non-empty server-side set-list completeness; live browser
acceptance of capability inspection, credential submission, and successful
authenticated navigation; complete question-level media
facts beyond the bounded presence currently observed; the outcome of
organization selection; Safari worker lifecycle. These prevent completion of
task 08 and publication tasks 09-12. Portable doubles are not browser
acceptance, and no live login, question read-back, or capability probe is
claimed.

Portable browser authentication is now implemented fail-closed. Question
listing remains a supported observational read: the worker navigates to the
exact set edit route, bounds the visible Edit-question controls, opens each
question, decodes the observed serialized question and normalized answer facts,
and verifies panel cleanup.

The question-list and Add Question read-back paths now require the same exact
set ID at every modal step, including opening, inspection, cancel, and confirmed
closure. A switched edit route or duplicate `id` query parameter fails closed
rather than accepting another set's question as the requested result. Portable
regressions cover the identity checks; no new live question-read claim follows.

The canonical question-list decoder also enforces the worker's 200-item local
read safety cap before traversing results. This is a resource bound, not a
claim about Blooket's account or question-count limits.
Oversized answer, correct-answer, and match-mode arrays likewise fail
before normalization, consistently with the existing 100-answer adapter guard.
Question number, time limit, prompt length, and answer text length also use
existing browser read bounds at the canonical decoder; these are local resource
limits rather than observations of provider account limits.

Answer-image, answer-math, and question-equation encodings now have bounded
reference-backed normalization, but question image/audio identity and broader
media behavior remain intentionally unclaimed.

The public Blooket identity login page observed on 2026-10-07 exposes the
visible heading `Log in`, inputs labeled by the placeholders `Username or email`
and `Password`, and one `Let's go!` button on `https://id.blooket.com/login`.
The curated create/edit/My Sets capture does not contain a login route, so this
public page observation is kept distinct from recovered build evidence.

The extension now grants only the additional `https://id.blooket.com/*` host
permission needed for that identity page. `session.observe` admits that origin
only for the exact login observation and maps a matching visible surface to
`signed-out`; other read operations still require the dashboard origin. Visible
CAPTCHA frames map to `security-challenge`, while other identity routes or
ambiguous login controls map to `unexpected-page` or fail closed.

`session.authenticate` now fills those exact visible controls through native
input setters, verifies the resulting values, and clicks `Let's go!` once. The
bridge broker holds the credential-bearing job only in bounded memory and the
extension returns no credential material. A successful submit reply does not
mean login succeeded: `ensureBlooketSession` performs the required fresh
observation and only then reports ready, wait, human-action, or authentication
failure. No challenge or organization choice is automated.

Recovered dashboard module 88289 bounds the organization prompt without making
it automatable. Search results become a selected organization only after a human
chooses one; the form then submits hidden `schoolId` with `action=school`. The
same form separately exposes `action=renounce` as the explicit "I'm not part of
a school or district" choice.

On server-action success the client closes the modal and calls
`router.refresh()`;
on error it keeps the flow in error handling. The recovered client does not
reveal the server-side account mutation or prove the navigation state after the
refresh. Because the two actions express different account assertions, the
worker continues to classify the prompt as `organization-prompt` and never
selects, renounces, or submits it automatically.

Recovered capability UI establishes the account-specific observation path. Edit
module 12048 reads `session.hasPlus` and passes it into the question editor.
Module 89770 always renders the answer-image control and the question Audio
control. The same `hasPlus` value selects the real answer-image picker and real
audio picker for Plus accounts, or the explicit Blooket Plus upsell drawers for
accounts without that entitlement.

Module 89770 renders explicit Cancel controls for the Add Question form and its
nested drawer; module 72398 confirms the parent form modal disables overlay
closure.

Portable capability inspection is now implemented behind the existing
`capabilities.inspect` browser bridge command. The worker selects one already
visible My Sets ID, opens Add Question without submitting it, opens only the
Audio drawer, resolves the shared `hasPlus` media gate, verifies cancellation of
the nested drawer and parent form, and restores the exact prior dashboard URL.
Any ambiguous control, malformed set row, cleanup failure, or restoration
failure invalidates the whole snapshot. The account-specific observation is
recorded as capability evidence without retaining a set ID or provider media
identity.

An account with no sets is handled without creating probe content: static
verified facts are returned while answer images and audio remain explicitly
`account-dependent`. The canonical read command, CLI, and MCP tool all use
read-only session inspection; signed-out or expired sessions return
`blooket-authentication-required` without reading stored credentials or invoking
browser authentication. Real Chrome/Safari acceptance of this capability probe
remains pending, so portable coverage is not promoted to live account evidence.

Curated dashboard HTML for My Sets, Create, and Edit each contains exactly one
visible Logout link to `https://id.blooket.com/logout`. Dashboard session
observation and set reads now require that recovered authenticated-shell marker
in addition to the route-specific navigation/form controls. Missing, duplicate,
or differently labeled logout controls become `unexpected-page` or a failed
read rather than being treated as authenticated. This deliberately does not
claim `expired-session`: the curated evidence does not distinguish expiry from
a normal signed-out redirect.

Live Chrome-for-Testing acceptance on 2026-10-07 exposed one login-observer bug:
Blooket's ordinary `/login` page mounts a visible reCAPTCHA anchor whose URL is
explicitly `size=invisible`. Treating every visible reCAPTCHA iframe as an
active challenge made the normal signed-out state unreachable.

Only on the login route, session observation and credential-form admission
ignore the observed Google reCAPTCHA `/recaptcha/api2/anchor` with one
`size=invisible` parameter.
Malformed frames, foreign origins, duplicate parameters, hCaptcha, and challenge
frames remain security stops. A second live run with the HEAD extension and
local broker classified the real login page as `signed-out`. Credential
submission and authenticated navigation remain separate acceptance work.

A later synthetic acceptance harness mistakenly called
`session.authenticate` with positional arguments instead of the required
single credential object.

That malformed command could not pass the version-one bridge request decoder,
so its broker timeout did not establish a login-host failure. The
broker now validates every request before queueing and rejects malformed jobs
with a stable browser failure, without dispatch or retry. Actual credential
submission and subsequent authenticated navigation remain unverified.

Recovered module 89770 further bounds the remaining question-media gap. The
question editor initializes separate image and audio strings plus an optional
math suffix embedded in `question` with the established math marker. Its media
dropzone is disabled whenever image, equation, or audio state is present, and
the shared delete path clears image/audio and removes the math suffix. This
establishes the editor's mutually exclusive media slot without establishing a
stable remote image/audio identity.

Question-read schema version three now separates that recovered prompt encoding
into plain `question` text plus nullable normalized `equation` LaTeX. Legacy v1
and v2 reads migrate the exact marker-delimited shape; malformed or ambiguous
markers fail closed. The concrete page extractor emits v3 directly and rejects
question image, audio, and equation states when more than one occupies the
recovered media slot. Differential verification includes equation state in its
baseline and cannot confirm a text-only write when an equation remains remote.

The provider-specific lowering and final Add Question page boundary also reject
math/image marker sequences in fields that are supposed to remain plain text,
so local text cannot silently acquire provider media semantics during a write.

Chrome scripting serializes an injected `func` without its module lexical
scope. Live login acceptance exposed that direct unit tests had hidden this
distinction: the previous login helpers and Add Question helpers referenced
module-local functions that were unavailable after injection.

Both flows now inject one self-contained action runner, while wrapper APIs
retain
their existing call shape. Regression tests reconstruct each runner from
`Function(toString())` before exercising it, so future browser-script closures
fail in portable tests instead of only in a live extension.
Recovered answer equations are likewise accepted only with the exact
writer-generated pair of math delimiters; repeated/internal delimiters fail
closed before normalized v3 data crosses the browser boundary.

Image and audio remain presence-only facts. Retaining their provider strings,
hashing them as substitute identities, or treating presence as content equality
would exceed the evidence and could falsely confirm a media write. The project
document still has no independent question-audio representation, so audio
identity/equivalence remains an explicit modeling/evidence blocker rather than
being guessed into the write plan.

Initial validation: 572 portable tests, strict TypeScript, browser-script
syntax, and compiled-extension assembly passed. Manual pairing controls were
removed at the human's request; that workflow is superseded by automatic local
workspace discovery, not a product requirement.

The human loaded the extension and reported Chrome registration status 3:
"Top-level await is disallowed in service workers." The worker startup was
corrected to register its listener synchronously, restore session storage
asynchronously, and queue popup actions behind restoration. The artifact test
now requires a synchronous emitted ESM dependency graph, which rejects this
class of startup failure.

The human then confirmed the popup and Disconnected state, establishing worker
message handling. After the automatic-discovery update, the human confirmed
Ready without entering any configuration.

A real extension read through the application adapters returned two IR-validated
set summaries and found the existing synthetic fixture. Its set-detail read also
passed the IR decoder and confirmed private visibility and the synthetic title,
without logging private lesson names or account values. No Blooket data was
changed.

Final portable checks pass 574 tests, strict TypeScript, and all three browser
script syntax checks. Emitted-worker tests require a synchronous module graph;
workspace relay tests reject unrelated origins, applications, malformed tokens,
and oversized responses. These were portable results; native package evidence
for subsequent revisions is recorded separately below.

The clean Linux archive for commit `eff0feb` subsequently passed extracted
package verification, including the updated automatic-discovery worker,
clipboard helper, icons, launcher, and native media preparation. This proves
that revision only; command composition received its own package check below.

Canonical read composition was implemented on 2026-10-06. The admitted
`blooket.session.inspect`, `blooket.sets.list`, and `blooket.sets.get` commands
share the existing validated application ports through the running local
service. Ordinary CLI syntax and the three matching MCP tools use that path; MCP
